`holdSeats` must check every requested seat and place the hold atomically — a hold that succeeds for 2 of 3 seats is worse than no hold, because it needs a compensating release.

```java
synchronized HoldResult holdSeats(List<String> seatIds, String userId) {
    for (String id : seatIds) {
        if (seatStates.get(id) != SeatState.AVAILABLE) {
            return HoldResult.failed(id);
        }
    }
    String holdId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
    Instant expiry = Instant.now().plus(HOLD_DURATION);
    for (String id : seatIds) {
        seatStates.put(id, SeatState.HELD);
        activeHolds.put(id, new Hold(holdId, userId, expiry));
    }
    return HoldResult.success(holdId, expiry);
}
```

This single-process `synchronized` version is a starting point, not the answer — the next slides show where it breaks.

Instead of a background sweeper polling every hold, check expiry lazily on the next access to that seat — whoever touches it next reclaims it if it's stale:

```java
SeatState effectiveState(String seatId) {
    SeatState state = seatStates.get(seatId);
    if (state == SeatState.HELD) {
        Hold hold = activeHolds.get(seatId);
        if (Instant.now().isAfter(hold.expiry)) {
            seatStates.put(seatId, SeatState.AVAILABLE);
            activeHolds.remove(seatId);
            return SeatState.AVAILABLE;
        }
    }
    return state;
}
```

No seat waits on a cron job to free up, and the one moment staleness could matter — right when someone else tries to book it — is exactly when this check runs.
